sep tember 1965 Helen Hadley hall I'm  beginning graduate studies in French and  I have a recurring dream Jean Bosch  director of graduate studies in French  calls me into his office and announces  in his genial way med was L we've  discovered that you were admitted to the  program by mistake we really intended to  admit someone else goodbye why should I  have such a nightmare well maybe in part  because it was the first time I'd ever  lived away from home as a college  student I lived with my family in  bensonhurst brooklyn and I commuted to  st. Joseph's to subway trains a bus and  a walk it was also the first time I'd  been in any school not run by the  sisters of st. Joseph and the first time  since eighth grade I'd been in a school  with boys a lot of us i'm sure we're  anxious but i think i may have had more  reason than most i was the child of a  father who did not finish high school  and of a mother who had a commercial  course that is to say she learned typing  and shorthand my three older siblings  all had either commercial or vocational  courses in high school and none of them  went beyond high school and in our 1965  yale entering cohort of about 40  students into the french program i was  one of just two who had never been to  France despite the nightmare and the  uncertainty it all worked out and the  five years I spent in New Haven the last  of them by the way in Timothy Dwight  College the year the college went co-ed  we're among the happiest of my life and  they were certainly the most formative  God knows I studied hard in fact I  reconnected last month with one of my  graduate school friends I hadn't seen  her but twice in the intervening 40  years and both times only briefly she  told me a story in her first year at  Yale she wasn't doing well academically  and she'd become despondent she claims  that I said this to her Ellen you have  to work harder you don't have to work as  hard as I do but you have to work harder  I was appalled I mean what evidence on  my part she however maintains that my  belief in her helped her to succeed  which i can add she did quite  brilliantly I don't remember playing  this role for her but I do remember  other students playing a similar role  for me the graduate student culture was  collaborative in my second year at Yale  as I was casting about for a paper topic  I asked a third-year student if he had  any ideas he was rushing off on a trip  but to my stupefaction two days later I  got a postcard this was in the time long  before email of course I got a postcard  listing five possible paper topics all  of them excellent I've saved that  postcard and I remain a friend of the  person who sent it to me who now works  by the way for a major foundation doing  good for countless aspiring young  professionals such as I was then i also  have a distinct memory of preparing for  PhD orals three of us worked together we  divided up the rather long list into  three parts and we each took primary  responsibility for one third reading all  the materials making notes and then  conferring that sort of group effort is  common today certainly it is at Hamilton  College we're on president but it was  not so common in the 1960s Bob Dawson  one of the three remained a friend until  his death in Paris a few years ago he  became a distinguished bibliophile after  he finished his Yale degree and over the  years the loan of rare books from his  private collection enabled almost all of  my own work the second of the three left  the profession to become a lawyer I  hadn't seen her in 20 years when last  year out of the blue as I was finishing  up a manuscript on women and aging in  18th century France I called her up and  asked her if she'd read it she said yes  she read it and she made wonderful  suggestions and proposed my very best  chapter title why am I telling you this  what I mean to say is that my fellow  Yale students smart quirky and generous  and able  my own success had the atmosphere had  been cut throat I would have shriveled  up and they've continued to play an  important role in my life in fact I  married one of them who became the most  constant and faithful of my supporters  then of course there were the teachers  zhang jie shalt know I'll be back Victor  Bombay Jean Bosch those men all of them  yes men taught me how to read and how to  think and especially George may my  dissertation director and incidentally  or perhaps not so incidentally the  dissertation director of my husband in  George mais seminar I made acquaintance  with an 18th century Dutch writer named  Isabel de shou yeah she is still not  exactly a household name except perhaps  in my own household I admire her  exemplary tolerance her largesse her  interest in educating youth and I admire  to the way she wrote her insistence on  simplicity and directness over  pretension or bombastic overstatement in  these attributes she resembles the  teachers I so loved at Yale when I  emulate those traits I'm most successful  in my current work educating the  students of Hamilton College many of  them first generation like me in  academia as in the world at large we  meet a rich diversity of people geniuses  prima donnas Saints mono maniacs none of  them has come as a surprise to me for I  learned to understand such characters  when I first encountered them in new  haven in the pages of malaya whoso vote  a about Zach post and Colette I will by  the way never stop believing that the  best preparation for any way of life is  the study of literature or one of the  other disciplines that a liberal  education offers my graduate my  gratitude rather to the Yale graduate  school is absolutely immense it took a  chance on a poor provincial naive girl  from Brooklyn it gave me a fellowship  and the best of education it gave me the  example of brilliant devoted teachers  who  spired me then and whose memory inspires  me still and have provided me with  friends who have never stopped  supporting me Thank You Yale
 

